[
    {
        "content": "<p>I got Claude to formalize an end-to-end proof that for the general graph we have a proof of implication or refutation between any two equations in our set (after applying transitivity and duality.) It does so using a certification approach, specifically, a python program generates a certificate that is then verified in Lean to optimize the kernel-checking. The certificate includes information about all SCCs of the graph, reachability between SCCs, a topological sort of SCCs, and information on refutations. The certificate is checked against a list of all implications and refutations (each entry in these lists includes the Lean proof from our repo.)</p>\n<p>The certification then:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Checks that the SCCs are well-formed. In the certificate, every SCC has a canonical representative equation and every equation has an implication chain to/from the SCCs canonical equation. This step verifies that these are well-formed (e.g. that the indices of the implications in the certificate actually match the implication in our trusted list.) This lets us reduce to considering SCCs.</li>\n<li>Check that every edge between SCCs respects the topological sort. This gives us a well-foundedness of recursive operations on the graph.</li>\n<li>The certificates stores which SCCs a given SCC implies and is implied by, this is just a pre-computed transitive closure. Verify that this transitive closure is correct by basically just checking that e.g. <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>∪</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> directly implies </mtext><mi>s</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">reaches(scc_i) = \\{scc_i\\} \\cup \\{reaches(scc_n) \\mid scc_i \\text{ directly implies } scc_n \\}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">e</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">es</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">sc</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">sc</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">∪</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0278em;\">r</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">e</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">h</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">es</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">sc</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∣</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">sc</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\"> directly implies </span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">sc</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">c</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span></span></span></span> and similarly for reached_by.</li>\n<li>The certificate also stores refutations as well, these need to similarly be verified against the refutation list.</li>\n<li>At this point, for every SCC we can take the transitive closure of its implications and refutations and verify that they cover the set of all SCCs.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Whenever we are doing set operations above, they are implemented as bitsets over Nats, so union is just boolean OR. This makes this fast to compute. Claude used <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"470149\">@Joachim Breitner</span>'s RArray implementation to make the kernel reduction fast, but I do not understand this part of Lean well enough to understand the specifics.</p>\n<p>In the end, we have a function which constructively returns a list of implications or a refutation between any pair of equations. From this we can derive the following corollaries which are basically the end-to-end proof stated in a different form:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lawOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Law</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">NatMagmaLaw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">laws</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">get</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Reachable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">checkChain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Refutable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∃</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">checkRefutation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">neg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">reachable_or_refutable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4694</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4694</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Reachable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∨</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Refutable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">neg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">implies_iff_reachable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4694</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hm</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4694</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">lawOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">implies</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">lawOf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Reachable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Where Reachable just takes a list of implications (as indices into the list of implications we've proven in the repository) and checks that they really do go from n to m, and Refutable takes a refutation which is two implications <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a \\Rightarrow n, m \\Rightarrow b</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇒</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">m</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇒</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span> and a witness satisfying <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span></span></span></span> but not <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>One current limitation is that this only works in Type 0 due to a limitation of <code>Law.MagmaLaw.implies</code> but I'm guessing this could be fixed without much work if we care about it. At the moment, the current version of the code adds about ~3m wall clock build (~16m cpu clock time) on an old 8-core laptop as well as a ~5 MB certificate. These could probably be optimized more, I have definitely not exhausted all options there. In total, there is less than &lt;1k lines of lean (excluding the auto-generated certificate which is basically all of the current ~5 MB increase.)</p>",
        "id": 622893081,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1788969357
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am not ready to PR it yet as I'd like to see if it could be optimized further and clean it up a bit more, but I hope that I can start PRing it by tomorrow.</p>",
        "id": 622893539,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1788969419
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://github.com/teorth/equational_theories/pull/1508\">equational#1508</a> is a trivial fix I broke out from the rest of the proof</p>",
        "id": 623124296,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1789041453
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The end-to-end proof is now down to &lt;2min wall clock (&lt;6m CPU) build time and the change is under 2 megabytes. The size of the lean code has increased to 1,300 lines since it now includes some meta-code to load JSON files more efficiently instead of generating huge tables.</p>",
        "id": 623149156,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1789046737
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The end-to-end proof is <a href=\"https://github.com/teorth/equational_theories/pull/1506\">equational#1506</a> I would really appreciate a review of the final statements in <code>Verified.lean</code> (You'll need to cross-reference <code>Basic.lean</code> for some definitions.) I have written the READMEs by hand, but the rest of the code is either AI-generated or the majority of <code>Generated/</code> is auto-generated by a script.</p>",
        "id": 623149597,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1789046829
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Could this code and certificate be used to obtain a statement of the theorem that could be loaded as is in later projects?  Maybe I'm not thinking the right way about this, but there are follow-up projects where I'd want to admit the results of the ETP and build on top of it without necessarily pulling the whole set of proofs.</p>",
        "id": 623751831,
        "sender_full_name": "Bruno Le Floch",
        "timestamp": 1789225018
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'll merge it as soon as consensus is reached</p>",
        "id": 623752480,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1789225540
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"466334\">Shreyas Srinivas</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/458659-Equational/topic/End-to-end.20proof/near/623752480\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I'll merge it as soon as consensus is reached</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes please hold off. I would really like to get at least 1 or 2 sets of eyes on just the Verification.lean statements to make sure others think its reasonable.</p>",
        "id": 623756598,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1789228988
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"773643\">Bruno Le Floch</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/458659-Equational/topic/End-to-end.20proof/near/623751831\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Could this code and certificate be used to obtain a statement of the theorem that could be loaded as is in later projects?  Maybe I'm not thinking the right way about this, but there are follow-up projects where I'd want to admit the results of the ETP and build on top of it without necessarily pulling the whole set of proofs.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You could add the equational theories project as a dependency and pull in the statement that way, but no you could not use the certificate verification without <em>also</em> pulling in all of the proofs. By necessity, the certificate check actually goes and checks that all of the referenced implications/refutations exist in the repository as stated. You could of course take whatever desired statement in Verification.lean you like (there are 3 basically equivalent but differently stated forms) and just take it as an axiom/hypothesis, but otherwise there's no way to get around proving all of theorems in the ETP.</p>",
        "id": 623756942,
        "sender_full_name": "Vlad Tsyrklevich",
        "timestamp": 1789229307
    }
]